--TEST--
The two halves of `Core\Xml`'s stream meet: what the writer escapes, the reader hands back unescaped, and the one construct the writer will not read back is named — `rule:core-classes/xml-tree-and-stream`, `rule:core-classes/xml-refuses-by-construction`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A writer builds a document a node at a time and a reader walks one a node at
// a time, so the strongest thing to ask is that they agree. Every character
// that would otherwise be markup is escaped by the member that took it — the
// program never writes an escape — and the walk on the other side sees the
// characters that went in.
class Walk {
    public static function over(string $document): void {
        Core\Xml\Reader $reader = Core\Xml::reader($document);
        while (true) {
            ?Core\Xml\Node $node = $reader->read();
            if ($node != null) {
                echo "  ", $reader->depth(), " \"", $node->name(), "\" \"", $node->text(), "\"\n";
            } else {
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}

Core\Xml\Writer $out = Core\Xml::writer();
$out->startDocument();
$out->comment("an order, written a node at a time");
$out->instruction("render", "mode=compact");
$out->startElement("order");
$out->attribute("id", "A&B \"7\"");
$out->startElement("line");
$out->content("2 < 3 & \"quoted\"");
$out->endElement();
$out->startElement("note");
$out->cdata("<em>as written</em>");
$out->endElement();
$out->startElement("empty");
$out->endElement();
$out->endElement();
string $document = $out->endDocument();
echo $document, "\n";
echo "read back:\n";
Walk::over($document);

// The one construct this class writes and will not read back. Naming a
// document type is not resolving one, so the writer has the member; the parser
// refuses a `<!DOCTYPE …>` whole rather than reading far enough to decide, so
// a document carrying one is written for a reader outside Novis.
Core\Xml\Writer $typed = Core\Xml::writer();
$typed->startDocument();
$typed->doctype("order");
$typed->startElement("order");
$typed->endElement();
echo $typed->endDocument(), "\n";
try {
    Core\Xml::parse("<!DOCTYPE order><order/>");
} catch (ParseError $refused) {
    echo "read back: ", $refused->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><!--an order, written a node at a time--><?render mode=compact?><order id="A&amp;B &quot;7&quot;"><line>2 &lt; 3 &amp; "quoted"</line><note><![CDATA[<em>as written</em>]]></note><empty/></order>
read back:
  0 "" "an order, written a node at a time"
  0 "render" "mode=compact"
  0 "order" ""
  1 "line" ""
  2 "" "2 < 3 & "quoted""
  1 "note" ""
  2 "" "<em>as written</em>"
  1 "empty" ""
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><!DOCTYPE order><order/>
read back: Core\Xml::parse(): a document type declaration is not read at all — no entity is resolved from an external subset, from an internal subset or from the network, so there is nothing a `<!DOCTYPE …>` could mean here.
